meta {
  name: Get Performance (Custom Range)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/performance?range=custom&from=2026-07-01&to=2026-07-31
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  range: custom
  from: 2026-07-01
  to: 2026-07-31
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("echoes back exactly the window requested", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().range).to.eql({
      key: "custom",
      from: "2026-07-01",
      to: "2026-07-31",
    });
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /performance?range=custom — an arbitrary window (PAC-9)

  Backs the dashboard's **📅 Custom Date** chip. Same endpoint and same response
  shape as the presets — one contract serves both, which is why the client can
  render a preset and a custom window through a single code path.

  - **Module / permission:** `performance` / `performance:read`

  `to` is **inclusive**: `from=2026-07-01&to=2026-07-31` covers all 31 days of
  July. The server converts that to a half-open `[20260701, 20260801)` range
  internally.

  The window is capped at **366 days**. That cap is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic: the aggregation accumulates distinct households with `$addToSet`,
  and the span is what bounds that set.

  Dates are **America/Chicago calendar days**, not instants — no timezone
  conversion is applied to what you send.

  ## Response `200`
  Identical to `Get Performance (This Month)`; only `range.key` differs.

  ## Errors
  - `400` — missing `from` or `to`, `from` after `to`, a non-existent date, or a
    span over 366 days. See `Get Performance (Invalid Custom)`.
}
